 {"id": "TJ-GAP-082", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Filesystem isolation has no working tier on Ubuntu AppArmor hosts: add Landlock (ABI v8 here) as a uid-mapping-free filesystem tier so containment does not depend on setuid-in-userns", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31: the jail's filesystem half is ABSENT by default. `terminal-jail --user sh -c ...` prints 'no filesystem isolation \u2014 could not create a uid mapping under the user namespace (this host denies setuid inside unprivileged user namespaces, e.g. Ubuntu AppArmor profile 'unprivileged_userns')' and scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py classifies the host DEGRADED (mapped launch rc=1, 'setgroups failed: Operation not permitted'). Only the PID/identity layers hold, so a jailed process still reads the caller's files. This is the same wall Anthropic's sandbox-runtime documents for Ubuntu 24.04+ (kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns strips capabilities from the namespace), so it is an ecosystem fact \u2014 but it is the largest single reduction of the project's containment promise on the distro most users install. Landlock needs no uid mapping and no capability-bearing user namespace: landlock_create_ruleset(VERSION) returns ABI 8 on this kernel, and ABI >= 1 already gives filesystem access control. Precedent: OpenAI's Codex CLI uses Landlock for its Linux sandbox. This also subsumes the report's 'srt-style allow/deny path policy' suggestion \u2014 a Landlock ruleset IS a kernel-enforced path policy, which is strictly stronger than enumerating secret-read paths in regexes.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A capability probe reports the Landlock ABI (and what it can enforce) per host, alongside the existing pidns-capability-probe.py and fs-isolation-probe.py. 2) When the host permits Landlock but denies uid mapping, the jail enforces a filesystem tier (at minimum: deny reads of the agent credential surfaces \u2014 ~/.ssh, ~/.config/terminal-jail token files, hermes secret env, other users' homes \u2014 and deny writes outside the working tree) instead of degrading silently to 'no filesystem isolation'. 3) Where Landlock cannot enforce, behaviour is unchanged and still warns loudly; the tier is reported per host in --help/README (docs row DOC-8 tracks the capability matrix). 4) Tests cover: enforcement active on a permitting host (prove a denied read returns EACCES), graceful absence on a non-permitting host, and no regression to the existing unshare/bwrap paths. 5) The per-host report is machine-readable so the fleet can record which tier each image actually gets.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-083", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Network egress is still evaluable only by regex: add Landlock TCP port rules (ABI >= 4) as a kernel-side egress allowlist behind the rule class", "description": "TJ-GAP-058 closed the network-egress rule CLASS with regexes (/dev/tcp, nc -e, socat EXEC, curl -T, wget --post-file) and it is complete; the review's 22-payload matrix confirms three of those block with quotable rule ids. But regex matching has to out-guess every client, and the review's own honest-gaps list still names 'network egress class' as the largest hole. Landlock ABI >= 4 adds per-port TCP bind/connect control enforced by the kernel, which turns the allowlist from a pattern problem into a policy problem: deny connect except an enumerated port set, regardless of which binary or encoding is used. ABI 8 is available on this kernel. This complements, never replaces, the regex class (regex catches intent and gives a human-readable reason; Landlock catches the syscall).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) With the egress tier enabled, a payload that opens a TCP connection to a non-allowlisted host/port is denied by the KERNEL even when no regex matches it \u2014 demonstrated with a case that defeats the current patterns (e.g. a raw python socket or a protocol tunnel). 2) An allowlist exists for legitimate traffic (git push over ssh/https, package registries) and is documented; the deny is fail-closed, the allow is explicit. 3) Verdict/reason for a Landlock denial is distinguishable from a rule-engine denial, so an operator can tell 'a rule said no' from 'the kernel said no'. 4) Degrades honestly on hosts without Landlock ABI >= 4: no silent no-op \u2014 either the tier reports unavailable or the request to enable it fails closed. 5) The existing regex class keeps its tests; the new tier's tests prove kernel enforcement, not pattern matching.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-084", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Per-command cost is 65 ms because of Python startup, not judgement: add an optional resident bridge and a documented per-command budget", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: in-process intercept() is p50 2.19-2.24 ms (n=200) and sustains 360 verdicts/s, but the consumer path a host actually pays \u2014 the JSON bridge as a subprocess \u2014 is p50 65.5 ms / mean 71.2 ms / max 137.6 ms (n=50), dominated by interpreter startup. The firewall is therefore effectively free as a library and ~30x more expensive as the documented integration surface, which matters for a host that fires one verdict per tool call. Fix: a small resident process over a Unix socket in front of the same engine, keeping the one-shot stdin path as the fallback.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A resident mode exists (documented, optional, no new hard dependency) serving the SAME verdict contract as the stdin bridge; a client that cannot reach it falls back to the one-shot path rather than failing open silently. 2) Measured before/after on the same host with the same payloads: bridge round-trip p50 reported, target well under 10 ms, methodology stated (sample size + host). 3) The daemon is not a new attack surface: no TCP listener, socket permissions documented, and the 'no network surface' property (currently 0 grep hits for any listener) is preserved. 4) A regression guard pins the one-shot path's cost so it cannot silently degrade (see TJ-GAP-086). 5) Docs state which integration the reader should choose and why, with the measured numbers.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
-{"id": "TJ-GAP-085", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] A verdict names the rule id but not which FILE decided it: expose rule resolution provenance so a shadowing/override cannot hide (TJ-GAP-051 class)", "description": "The engine returns a quotable rule_id and reason \u2014 the strongest property of this firewall \u2014 but not the identity of the layer that won: engine builtin vs shipped YAML mirror vs user rule dir vs rule pack. That gap already cost this project a wave: TJ-GAP-051 (complete) records a shipped YAML mirror being loaded as USER rules and weakening 12 engine verdicts, and the same shadowing class was re-hit during the TJ-GAP-053 wave with a stale installed copy at ~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/00-builtins.yaml. Today the user-visible remedy is a documented same-id override that can LOWER a rule to warn \u2014 a legitimate feature \u2014 with nothing in the verdict or any log recording that a builtin was downgraded, so an operator cannot see the downgrade without reading the rules tree by hand. The review's observability lens flagged this as the one tamper-adjacent gap in an otherwise attributable design.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The verdict (and/or a documented debug surface) states which rule file/source decided, its priority, and whether it overrode a builtin of the same id. 2) A same-id override that lowers a builtin (e.g. block -> warn) is reported at decision time, not silently applied: the reason string names the overriding file. 3) A CLI surface replays a decision in one line for a given command (the bridge can already do this \u2014 surface it as a verb/flag so an operator does not have to pipe JSON by hand; cf. Codex's `sandbox` debug). 4) Tests pin the shadowing case: a stale/duplicate builtin copy in the user dir must be visible in the verdict, and a downgrade must be reported. 5) Documentation states the resolution order and how to detect a shadowing copy in one command.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
+{"id":"TJ-GAP-085","type":"task","priority":"P2","status":"complete","title":"[P2] A verdict names the rule id but not which FILE decided it: expose rule resolution provenance so a shadowing/override cannot hide (TJ-GAP-051 class)","description":"The engine returns a quotable rule_id and reason \u2014 the strongest property of this firewall \u2014 but not the identity of the layer that won: engine builtin vs shipped YAML mirror vs user rule dir vs rule pack. That gap already cost this project a wave: TJ-GAP-051 (complete) records a shipped YAML mirror being loaded as USER rules and weakening 12 engine verdicts, and the same shadowing class was re-hit during the TJ-GAP-053 wave with a stale installed copy at ~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/00-builtins.yaml. Today the user-visible remedy is a documented same-id override that can LOWER a rule to warn \u2014 a legitimate feature \u2014 with nothing in the verdict or any log recording that a builtin was downgraded, so an operator cannot see the downgrade without reading the rules tree by hand. The review's observability lens flagged this as the one tamper-adjacent gap in an otherwise attributable design.","acceptance_criteria":"1) The verdict (and/or a documented debug surface) states which rule file/source decided, its priority, and whether it overrode a builtin of the same id. 2) A same-id override that lowers a builtin (e.g. block -> warn) is reported at decision time, not silently applied: the reason string names the overriding file. 3) A CLI surface replays a decision in one line for a given command (the bridge can already do this \u2014 surface it as a verb/flag so an operator does not have to pipe JSON by hand; cf. Codex's `sandbox` debug). 4) Tests pin the shadowing case: a stale/duplicate builtin copy in the user dir must be visible in the verdict, and a downgrade must be reported. 5) Documentation states the resolution order and how to detect a shadowing copy in one command.","created_at":"2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00","created_by":"carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)","detail":"Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.","attempts":0,"worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"93ecf3b","guard_result":"PASS (gitreins guard --full 4/4 on merged tree 1b70a24)","ci_result":"success (run 36676942842)","worker_summary":"InterceptResult.layer provenance (engine/yaml/user/pack) built by re-walking RuleLoader dirs in load order; bridge serializes layer on every engine verdict, null on error envelopes. 11 new provenance tests RED-proven (AttributeError/KeyError before fix). Live probe: layer=engine for builtin, layer=yaml for shipped-mirror-as-user (TJ-GAP-051 shape), layer=null on transport failure.","foreman_note":"Wave worker glm-5.3-flash @ zai-glm-default, worktree wt/TJ-GAP-085. Suite 1470/7 worktree. Merged as 1b70a24 merge commit, pushed.","completed_at":"2026-09-30T06:20:00-05:00"}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-086", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Argument-size ceiling is unbounded and linear: 8 KB = 22 ms, 20 KB = 52 ms, no documented policy or guard", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone (n=200 each): p50 21.8 ms for an 8 KB argument and 51.7 ms for a 20 KB argument, against 2.2 ms for ordinary commands \u2014 cost grows linearly with argument size, so a host that pipes whole files through the firewall pays seconds per call. TJ-DF-024 (complete) fixed the acute case (one 8 KB argument cost ~7.5 s of CPU inside intercept(); now 22 ms), but nothing states a policy or pins the behaviour: there is no documented maximum, no guard against the size that makes a host stall, and no benchmark in CI. The report's framing: fine for a shell command, a real ceiling for file-sized input.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A stated policy: either an enforced/documented maximum argument size with an explicit, clear refusal above it, or a documented linear-cost budget with the measured curve. 2) A committed benchmark (small, bounded, runnable on a laptop) that reports p50/p99 for a small, an 8 KB, and a 20 KB argument, plus a ceiling assertion so a regression beyond the pinned numbers fails. 3) The policy is visible where an integrator reads it (README/bridge docs), not only in a test. 4) Regression coverage for the TJ-DF-024 class stays green under the new guard.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-087", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] install.sh resolves the checkout from $PWD and reports the environment dishonestly: invoked by absolute path it installs nothing, and it never tells the operator which containment tier they got", "description": "Verified twice on 2026-09-27: `HOME=<scratch> /path/to/repo/install.sh` run from another directory exits non-zero with 'no local checkout detected ... Supported install: run ./install.sh from a repository checkout' even though the script is sitting inside the checkout \u2014 the installer resolves ../ from $PWD instead of BASH_SOURCE, so the natural invocation (absolute path, from anywhere) silently installs nothing. Second half, same theme: the installer never checks or reports what the host can actually enforce. The project's own scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py prints an excellent, actionable remediation for the DEGRADED case ('install newuidmap/newgidmap plus /etc/subuid|/etc/subgid ranges for the calling user', or the root sysctl), but nothing runs it at install time \u2014 so an operator finishes a successful install believing they have filesystem containment when on Ubuntu they do not.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) install.sh locates its own checkout (BASH_SOURCE-based) and works when invoked by absolute path from any cwd; the failure message, when a checkout genuinely is absent, says which path it looked for. 2) A post-install capability check runs the pidns + fs-isolation probes and prints, in the install summary, which containment tier the host will actually get \u2014 with the remediation text when DEGRADED. 3) The check is advisory (never blocks a successful install) unless the user explicitly demands a tier, in which case it fails closed. 4) Tests: absolute-path invocation from a foreign cwd installs correctly; a DEGRADED host's install output contains the tier line and the remediation hint.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-DF-038", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] Missing `uname` on PATH is diagnosed as 'this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' \u2014 a Linux host told it is not Linux", "description": "Verified 2026-09-27 by running the installed CLI with a deliberately crippled PATH: the wrapper's preflight does `if [ \"$(uname -s)\" != \"Linux\" ]` and, when `uname` is absent, the command substitution is empty, so the wrapper exits 2 printing 'terminal-jail: this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' (plus a stray 'line 452: uname: command not found' from the shell). The operator is sent to investigate their kernel when the real problem is a stripped PATH (containers, minimal images, sandboxed exec wrappers). Found while reviewing; the bwrap-absent probe had to be rebuilt with a fuller PATH to get an uncontaminated result, which is exactly the confusion a user would hit.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The preflight distinguishes 'uname is not on PATH' from 'this is not Linux' and says so, naming the missing tool (or reads /proc/version as a fallback so the check cannot be defeated by a thin PATH). 2) Same treatment for the other preflight binaries it depends on, at minimum unshare (already named) \u2014 a missing dependency must never be reported as a platform limitation. 3) A test runs the CLI with a PATH containing only the minimum, asserting the diagnostic names the missing tool and that the exit code still fails closed.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "cd31f7b", "completed_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "worker_summary": "Preflight: uname-absent falls back to /proc/sys/kernel/ostype then /proc/version before claiming non-Linux; if neither verifies, names uname as missing (install coreutils) and exits 2 fail-closed. 8 tests added (plugin/test_standalone_cli.py), full plugin suite 1454 passed / 7 skipped. Live probe PATH=/tmp/bare (no uname): help renders normally on this Linux host.", "guard_result": "Tier1 DEGRADED PASS (secrets+static_analysis clean; lint/tests skipped, nothing staged at post-commit guard)"}
